EXT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE, PRAIRIE JUNCTION - DAY

A single-room telegraph shack beside the railroad water tower, its porch sagging. Heat shimmers off the tracks. A pinto horse is tied to the rail, flicking flies.

Inside the open door, EZEKIEL "ZEKE" MORROW (50s, stationmaster, sleeve garters, thick spectacles) sits at the key. The sounder chatters. He copies down a message, then looks up as hoofbeats thunder in. TOMAS ESQUIVEL (20s, deputy, hat low) swings off his horse, ties it badly, and bursts onto the porch, while behind him a lean stranger, WILHELMINA "BILLIE" TATE (30s, drover's coat, a rifle in the crook of her arm) rides up slowly and stays in the saddle, watching the street.

TOMAS
Zeke! Is it true?

ZEKE
Sit down, son.

TOMAS
Is it true they're coming here?

The sounder chatters again. Zeke raises a hand for quiet, writes, and then reads aloud.

ZEKE
Tower says the Harlan gang crossed the river an hour ago.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
(over the wire, read aloud by Zeke)
Hold the junction. Do not engage. Repeat, do not engage.

TOMAS
Do not engage? They'll burn the depot.

Billie dismounts, walks up the steps, and without a word takes the telegram from Zeke's hand and reads it. Tomas stares at her rifle. Zeke takes off his spectacles and wipes them on his sleeve.

BILLIE
Where's the sheriff?

TOMAS
Dead. Fever, three days back.

BILLIE
Then you're the law.

TOMAS
I'm twenty-two.

BILLIE
Today you're the law.

A rider appears at the end of the street, then another, then five. Dust rises behind them. Tomas draws his pistol and his hand shakes. Zeke slides under the desk. Billie raises the rifle, levels it, and fires once. The lead horse rears, and the riders scatter behind the water tower.

TOMAS
You hit him!

BILLIE
I hit the horse. Don't thank me yet.

Silence. Then a white handkerchief appears on a rifle barrel from behind the tower.

ZEKE
(from under the desk)
Is that what I think it is?

BILLIE
Somebody wants to talk.

Tomas lets out a breath that is almost a laugh. Billie lowers the rifle but does not unload it.
